WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:06.440
In this part of our presentation, we're going to be taking a look at ways to optimize our

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.440
textures.

3
00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.720
The first texture optimization we're going to talk about is making sure that streaming

4
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.239
virtual textures or SVT, which is an alternative system for streaming textures from disk,

5
00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:19.579
is enabled.

6
00:00:19.579 --> 00:00:25.600
In all new Unreal Engine 5.5 projects, SVT is enabled by default, but if you're

7
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:29.399
in a previous version of Unreal, you can come up here to your project settings, rendering,

8
00:00:29.399 --> 00:00:33.159
then under virtual textures, enable virtual texture support.

9
00:00:33.159 --> 00:00:37.170
Once you've done that, you will need to restart the editor.

10
00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:44.310
Streaming virtual textures work slightly differently than the traditional MIP map based streaming,

11
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:49.670
in the sense that when Unreal uses the standard MIP based streaming, it has to load the entire

12
00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:55.390
texture for an object, even if a single pixel of that object is visible on screen.

13
00:00:55.390 --> 00:01:00.350
While this method works, it adds unnecessary memory overhead since the full texture data

14
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:06.040
must be streamed in, regardless of how much of it is actually visible.

15
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.079
Streaming virtual texturing solves this problem by only streaming in parts of a texture

16
00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:12.719
that are needed for what's currently visible.

17
00:01:12.719 --> 00:01:17.439
It does this by dividing all MIP levels into small, fixed-sized tiles.

18
00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:22.599
The GPU then determines exactly which tiles are visible on screen and streams in those

19
00:01:22.599 --> 00:01:24.569
tiles only.

20
00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:28.849
This means that no matter how large the texture is, only the visible tiles are loaded into

21
00:01:28.849 --> 00:01:34.069
memory, which keeps performance and memory usage more consistent.

22
00:01:34.069 --> 00:01:39.430
Again, to start using streaming virtual textures in your project, all you need to do is make

23
00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:46.200
sure that you have virtual texture support enabled, and then restart the editor.

24
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.090
Once that's done, Unreal will automatically handle virtual texture streaming and processing

25
00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:54.760
for your assets.

26
00:01:55.159 --> 00:02:00.019
Now, there are some options that we need to make sure in order to get the best out

27
00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:06.500
of this system, and the first one is the pool config for either fixed pools or transient

28
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:10.900
pools for your virtual textures.

29
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.939
This section controls how much memory is allocated for virtual textures during runtime.

30
00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:20.400
So again, we've said there's two types of memory pools, there's the fixed pool and

31
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.400
the transient pool.

32
00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:25.080
The fixed pools are serialized settings stored with the project.

33
00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:30.639
These are evaluated from the last to first, and Unreal uses the first configuration that

34
00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:32.080
matches the system setup.

35
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.199
These values persist between editor sessions, meaning they remain consistent every time

36
00:02:37.199 --> 00:02:39.360
the project is open.

37
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.719
Transient pools, on the other hand, are created dynamically at runtime.

38
00:02:42.719 --> 00:02:48.039
They are used by the pool auto-grow system to adjust virtual texture memory as needed.

39
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.319
A virtual texture physical pool will first search these transient configs for a match before

40
00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:55.919
checking the fixed settings.

41
00:02:55.919 --> 00:03:00.800
Transient pools only exist for current editor session, but they can be copied over to the

42
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.560
fixed pool later.

43
00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:09.110
This is a useful way to estimate proper memory pool sizes for a cooked project.

44
00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:14.289
Each pool description inside fixed pools array includes details such as textures, formats,

45
00:03:14.289 --> 00:03:15.889
and tile size.

46
00:03:15.889 --> 00:03:19.250
You can fine tune these settings to match the needs of your project.

47
00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.370
For example, you can set virtual texture memory pool to contain DTX1 textures to a

48
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:31.319
fixed size of 100MB by expanding the pool index and adjusting the size in MB field.

49
00:03:31.319 --> 00:03:36.159
These settings help balance texture quality, streaming, stability, and memory performance

50
00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:41.069
across different hardware targets.

51
00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:47.069
Now when you are working with virtual textures inside of your project, there's a couple

52
00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:48.909
of things that you need to be aware of.

53
00:03:48.909 --> 00:03:56.610
First off, any texture that is brought into Unreal can be used with streaming virtual

54
00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.610
textures.

55
00:03:57.610 --> 00:04:00.110
You just need to make sure that it is converted to a virtual texture.

56
00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:05.710
You'll be notified that a texture is a virtual texture, as it will have the little VT on

57
00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:10.379
the side of the texture inside of the content browser.

58
00:04:10.379 --> 00:04:16.199
Once you've imported it, when you're using it inside of a material, you'll want to make

59
00:04:16.199 --> 00:04:20.870
sure that the sampler type is set appropriately.

60
00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:26.490
The available virtual samples are virtual color, virtual grayscale, virtual alpha, normal,

61
00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:33.670
mask, linear color, and virtual linear color grayscale.

62
00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:37.790
Choosing the correct sampler type ensures that Unreal handles the textured data properly

63
00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.189
when streaming tiles in and out at runtime.

64
00:04:41.189 --> 00:04:45.550
You can quickly tell whether a texture is a virtual texture or not by clicking on

65
00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:51.279
its thumbnail in the content browser, and you'll see the little VT there.

66
00:04:51.279 --> 00:04:57.500
One of the many techniques Unreal Engine 5 employs in order to help reduce texture

67
00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:03.439
overhead is something called MIP mapping, which is a technique where a texture's resolution

68
00:05:03.439 --> 00:05:07.000
is scaled down into multiple smaller versions of itself.

69
00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:11.319
We can actually see an example of that on our screen right here.

70
00:05:11.319 --> 00:05:17.279
Here's a texture that was imported at 4096 by 4096, and we can see the MIP maps for

71
00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:18.279
it.

72
00:05:18.279 --> 00:05:22.160
Now, Unreal will automatically generate these MIP maps for you if your texture maintains

73
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.519
a power of two ratio in the X and the Y axis.

74
00:05:25.519 --> 00:05:28.199
We'll dive into that a little bit further in our next slide.

75
00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:34.560
But for now, the main thing to understand here is that all we need to do in order

76
00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:42.160
to make sure this optimization takes place is that our texture is imported using this

77
00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:44.079
power of two rule.

78
00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:49.480
Once we have done that, Unreal will automatically do the rest for us.

79
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:53.439
Now, there are a few options that we have.

80
00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:57.279
You can see here to the right, I have this from a texture group, simple average,

81
00:05:57.279 --> 00:06:02.540
and there's some sharpen and then no MIPs, leave existing MIPs, blur and unfiltered.

82
00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:09.339
What these do is they allow us to adjust the MIP generation so that we can either make

83
00:06:09.339 --> 00:06:13.189
things a little sharper or make them a little blurry.

84
00:06:13.189 --> 00:06:18.230
I very rarely have to adjust these, but in instances such as foliage or maybe something

85
00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:24.870
like a chain link fence or small ornate detail, you might need to run a sharpen just

86
00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.069
to get some of that detail back.

87
00:06:27.069 --> 00:06:32.670
Now again, like I said, Unreal will automatically do this MIP generation for us, but your

88
00:06:32.689 --> 00:06:35.250
texture needs to meet certain requirements.

89
00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:40.689
Now, MIP mapping does work for both streamed and virtual textures, but we need to make

90
00:06:40.689 --> 00:06:45.730
sure that our texture is using a power of two in the X and the Y axis.

91
00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:54.089
This could be something like 64 by 64 or 256 by 256, even 512, 1024, 2048, 4096,

92
00:06:54.089 --> 00:06:56.810
and all the way up to 8192.

93
00:06:56.810 --> 00:07:00.250
That's not to say that you can't use non-power of two textures.

94
00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:04.629
We actually have an example of that down here, this imported at 1000 by 1000.

95
00:07:04.629 --> 00:07:09.029
It just means that if you use a texture that is a non-power of two, it is not going

96
00:07:09.029 --> 00:07:13.470
to make use of this MIP map system, and it's always going to be displayed at its

97
00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.689
highest resolution, no matter where it's viewed in the world.

98
00:07:17.689 --> 00:07:22.750
Let's just take a little closer look at some of the things we can quickly use to identify

99
00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:27.329
textures that are imported properly.

100
00:07:27.329 --> 00:07:32.089
This here on the left-hand side is taken from the texture viewing tool, which is activated

101
00:07:32.089 --> 00:07:34.829
just by double-clicking on a texture and opening it up.

102
00:07:34.829 --> 00:07:41.930
We can see here imported 1024 by 1024, method streamed, resource size 704, number of MIPs 11.

103
00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:46.589
This number of MIPs in this method stream, this is signifying that this particular texture

104
00:07:46.589 --> 00:07:53.970
right here is set up to be used with the MIP map based texture streaming system.

105
00:07:54.230 --> 00:08:00.209
Next, the exact same texture, we see the same thing imported 1024 by 1024, method virtual streamed,

106
00:08:00.209 --> 00:08:04.050
that's good, resource size 2KB, number of MIPs 11.

107
00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:09.329
So what this means is that this particular texture right here will use the virtual texture

108
00:08:09.329 --> 00:08:10.910
streaming system.

109
00:08:10.910 --> 00:08:15.490
It does have 11 MIP maps and currently occupying only 2KB, and that's because of how the

110
00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:21.459
virtual texture streaming system cuts up textures.

111
00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.500
Last but not least, again, this texture imported 1000 by 1000, we see here method not streamed,

112
00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:29.500
resource size 512 by 512, number of MIPs 1.

113
00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.620
What this is signifying to us is that this texture right here, no matter how far it's

114
00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:40.299
viewed from the camera or how close, it's always going to occupy this 512 KB of memory.

115
00:08:40.299 --> 00:08:45.929
Now if you're still a little not sure about what MIP maps are actually doing, here's

116
00:08:45.929 --> 00:08:51.059
an example of turning MIP mapping on and off.

117
00:08:51.059 --> 00:08:55.600
We can see here with MIP mapping on, as the texture gets further and further away from

118
00:08:55.600 --> 00:09:00.580
the camera, off into the distance, it's really easy for us to see it kind of making

119
00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:05.659
this pattern, we don't kind of get lost, there isn't this really kind of soupy pattern.

120
00:09:05.659 --> 00:09:10.299
Now with MIP mapping off, what we're doing is we're again taking the exact same texture,

121
00:09:10.299 --> 00:09:15.659
we're not reducing it in any size whatsoever, we are just copying it the exact same

122
00:09:15.659 --> 00:09:20.299
size and the distance, and as you can see, it gets really hard to read the details,

123
00:09:20.539 --> 00:09:26.259
it gets really smushed, it just, it looks like a little bit of just line soup, it is

124
00:09:26.259 --> 00:09:31.620
very very hard to read, so again, we get this really great advantage of using MIP mapping.

125
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.879
Not only is it done for us automatically behind the scenes, but it also helps manage

126
00:09:35.879 --> 00:09:42.100
some visual stuff, making very very distant objects kind of merge together, looking more

127
00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.730
natural.

128
00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:50.889
Now the last thing that I want to mention about MIP maps has to do with texture size.

129
00:09:50.889 --> 00:09:58.179
Now, you may be tempted to use the highest resolution texture you possibly can, and

130
00:09:58.179 --> 00:10:00.940
I want you to resist this.

131
00:10:00.940 --> 00:10:07.559
Oftentimes, setting a texture to 4K or 8K means that texture will only actually be

132
00:10:07.559 --> 00:10:11.460
seen when it is very very close to the camera.

133
00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:19.330
In this example, we can see here the MIP mapping texture that was created before, placed on

134
00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:23.250
objects in a world where the camera is positioned kind of far away.

135
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:28.289
We notice that the 4096 version isn't actually used that much.

136
00:10:28.289 --> 00:10:35.149
What is used a lot though, is the 2048, the 512, 1024, and so on, so we can see that

137
00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:40.129
even though this texture goes all the way up to 4K, that 4K version of the texture

138
00:10:40.129 --> 00:10:42.210
isn't used in a lot of locations.

139
00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:49.399
In fact, most of this scene is comprised of 1024 and lower.

140
00:10:49.399 --> 00:10:53.379
When dealing with alpha channel information inside of Unreal Engine 5, there's two ways

141
00:10:53.379 --> 00:10:54.419
to approach this.

142
00:10:54.419 --> 00:10:58.740
The first way is to use an embedded alpha, which means the alpha information is stored

143
00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:03.039
in the alpha channel of the texture it's related to.

144
00:11:03.039 --> 00:11:05.419
The second way is to do something called a separate alpha.

145
00:11:05.419 --> 00:11:10.220
This is where we take the alpha information and we put it inside of a different texture.

146
00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.360
That way, when we bring it into Unreal, we're actually going to have two textures.

147
00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:18.240
We're going to have the base color texture, and then the alpha texture that goes

148
00:11:18.279 --> 00:11:20.600
with that particular base color.

149
00:11:20.600 --> 00:11:25.559
Each method has its own unique advantages and disadvantages.

150
00:11:25.559 --> 00:11:30.399
For example, if we're using an embedded alpha, that's going to give us a texture that's

151
00:11:30.399 --> 00:11:32.399
around 1.3 megabytes.

152
00:11:32.399 --> 00:11:36.440
The reason for this is that any information we put inside the alpha channel, when brought

153
00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:40.159
into Unreal Engine 5, will remain uncompressed.

154
00:11:40.159 --> 00:11:44.639
If we need to have a high resolution alpha for a mask or something like that, by embedding

155
00:11:44.639 --> 00:11:49.600
it into the alpha channel, when we bring that into Unreal Engine 5, we can access the

156
00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.639
full resolution of that data.

157
00:11:52.639 --> 00:11:58.179
If we want to store the alpha information inside of a separate texture, what this is

158
00:11:58.179 --> 00:12:03.220
actually going to do is it's going to bring down the size of our base color texture

159
00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.419
to about half the size.

160
00:12:04.419 --> 00:12:10.100
So that same 1024 by 1024 texture with the embedded alpha that was 1.33 megabytes will

161
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:18.509
actually only be around 682 kilobytes of information, or again, about half.

162
00:12:18.509 --> 00:12:22.509
Remember, the reason that it's half is that alpha information stored in the embedded

163
00:12:22.509 --> 00:12:25.539
alpha is uncompressed.

164
00:12:25.539 --> 00:12:31.620
The method that you choose for your project will depend on the needs for your alphas.

165
00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:37.299
I recommend using the separate alpha because it has a couple of workflow advantages that

166
00:12:37.299 --> 00:12:42.100
the embedded alpha does not such as being able to store multiple alpha masks and the

167
00:12:42.100 --> 00:12:44.860
red, green, and blue channels.

168
00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:50.899
The only other piece of advice I have for this is that if you do pick one method, embedded

169
00:12:50.899 --> 00:12:55.860
versus separate, you should continue to use that method throughout the lifetime of

170
00:12:55.860 --> 00:12:56.980
your projects.

171
00:12:56.980 --> 00:13:04.379
Don't mix and match as this can make debugging and finding out which textures are

172
00:13:04.379 --> 00:13:10.379
very large and consuming the most memory much more difficult to come to a proper way

173
00:13:10.379 --> 00:13:13.360
to optimize.

174
00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:19.039
In this example right here, I want to dive a little bit more into the embedded versus

175
00:13:19.039 --> 00:13:23.919
separate alpha so you can understand more why you might choose one versus the other.

176
00:13:23.919 --> 00:13:27.679
But to do this, we're going to go over to the editor and have a look at our two

177
00:13:27.679 --> 00:13:28.480
examples here.

178
00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:31.360
So here we have our embedded and our separate.

179
00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:36.159
Let's grab the embedded and move over to it and then double click on its material so

180
00:13:36.159 --> 00:13:37.519
we can see the texture.

181
00:13:37.519 --> 00:13:41.659
So I'm going to click on this texture right here and let's say that somebody comes

182
00:13:41.659 --> 00:13:45.980
to us and says, hey, we need to reduce the size of all of our foliage textures

183
00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:49.500
from their current size of 512 by 512 down to 256.

184
00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:53.259
So if we come here to our LOD bias and we set this to a value of one, it is going

185
00:13:53.259 --> 00:13:54.539
to bring everything down.

186
00:13:54.539 --> 00:14:00.460
But notice if we do take a look at this, it has reduced the size, but it's also reduced

187
00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:01.419
the visual quality.

188
00:14:01.419 --> 00:14:05.740
So if I was to take this back to zero, you can see here looks like a branch.

189
00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:07.580
Looks like a low resolution branch, right?

190
00:14:08.299 --> 00:14:11.980
Now, if I close this and come over here to our separate alpha, check this out.

191
00:14:11.980 --> 00:14:14.779
I'm going to grab on this, open up the material.

192
00:14:14.779 --> 00:14:17.259
Now I have these two separate textures.

193
00:14:17.259 --> 00:14:18.700
I have the alpha texture and the base color.

194
00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:22.750
Let's go ahead and open up the alpha and it's already set to one.

195
00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:25.149
So let me just undo that so we can have a look at this to begin with.

196
00:14:25.149 --> 00:14:28.750
So this is what it looks like with that set to zero.

197
00:14:28.750 --> 00:14:33.309
Let's bring this down to a value of one and notice pretty much no change, actually.

198
00:14:34.649 --> 00:14:42.409
There is a little bit of change, but overall, things are looking really, really good.

199
00:14:42.409 --> 00:14:44.330
So we've reduced this quite a lot.

200
00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:48.809
And that's really the distinct advantage between these two is that I have now independent

201
00:14:48.809 --> 00:14:56.750
control over reducing the alpha information separately from the base color.

202
00:14:56.750 --> 00:15:00.669
Now, there is one thing that I would like to point out in later versions of Unreal.

203
00:15:00.669 --> 00:15:06.750
If we come over here to this embedded alpha and we open up this, there is a property in

204
00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:15.899
here called Alpha Coverage Thresholds.

205
00:15:15.899 --> 00:15:25.019
And what this will do is this will allow us to adjust the alpha mask intensity so

206
00:15:25.019 --> 00:15:31.500
we can set this to help make the alpha mask more intense as we bring the size of

207
00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:34.779
that texture down to compensate for any loss of detail.

208
00:15:34.779 --> 00:15:37.500
All we need to do is simply input some numbers here.

209
00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:40.779
We don't want to go all the way up to something like one.

210
00:15:40.779 --> 00:15:46.620
We want to keep these numbers between, say, 0.5 and 0.9.

211
00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:55.399
So, you know, just set and see what's right for the textures inside of your project.

212
00:15:56.809 --> 00:16:01.370
In this part of texture optimizations, I'd like to talk about RGB mask packing.

213
00:16:02.009 --> 00:16:07.129
RGB mask packing is a technique used to reduce the number of textures and overall memory footprint

214
00:16:07.129 --> 00:16:08.009
in your project.

215
00:16:08.009 --> 00:16:12.490
It works by storing different grayscale textures in the red, green, blue, and sometimes

216
00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.809
alpha channels of a single texture file.

217
00:16:15.529 --> 00:16:21.129
Inside your material, you add the RGB packed texture to the material graph and then pull

218
00:16:21.129 --> 00:16:27.289
from the RGB or A output pins to access the specific data stored in each channel.

219
00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:33.309
This allows you to use one texture for multiple types of information instead of importing

220
00:16:33.309 --> 00:16:34.590
several separate textures.

221
00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:41.389
RGB mask packing is most commonly used for workflows that involve masks or VFX textures

222
00:16:41.389 --> 00:16:46.190
where each channel contains black and white data representing something such as roughness,

223
00:16:46.190 --> 00:16:49.070
metallic, ambient occlusion, or some type of opacity.

224
00:16:51.769 --> 00:16:58.649
When using any type of RGB packed texture, you need to make sure that sRGB is disabled.

225
00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:03.850
This can be accomplished by setting your compression settings on the texture to mask

226
00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:08.650
or by going to texture sRGB and disabling the checkmark box.

227
00:17:10.039 --> 00:17:14.680
The reason this is so important can be seen in the following slide.

228
00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:21.480
On the left, we have sRGB enabled and on the right, we have sRGB disabled.

229
00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:25.960
And at first look, it might be a little bit hard to tell the difference between the

230
00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:30.599
two, but if we zoom in, we can start to see what is actually going on.

231
00:17:30.599 --> 00:17:34.519
First off, on the zoomed in version, if we look at some of these leaves here,

232
00:17:34.519 --> 00:17:37.640
we're actually missing quite a bit of leaf detail.

233
00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:39.319
In fact, we're missing some right here.

234
00:17:39.319 --> 00:17:41.240
We're missing all of this right here.

235
00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:43.000
There's just a lot that is missing.

236
00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:47.799
And if we look with sRGB disabled, notice a lot of that detail is back.

237
00:17:47.799 --> 00:17:50.039
We actually have this entire branch back.

238
00:17:50.680 --> 00:17:52.519
We have just a lot more detail.

239
00:17:52.519 --> 00:17:54.519
We're seeing a lot more information.

240
00:17:54.519 --> 00:17:59.319
And the reason for this is that sRGB is enabling or disabling

241
00:17:59.319 --> 00:18:01.799
gamma correction on your texture.

242
00:18:02.359 --> 00:18:07.240
Since a masked texture is not used to show color information,

243
00:18:07.240 --> 00:18:10.440
but tell the renderer where something should or should not be,

244
00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:15.240
we want to make sure that we are not changing the values inside that texture.

245
00:18:16.200 --> 00:18:23.400
With sRGB disabled, again, the editor is not using that texture

246
00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:27.160
and gamma correcting it, leaving the values at what they are authored at.

247
00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:34.299
And the reason that we want to do this is so that when we use that masked texture,

248
00:18:34.299 --> 00:18:40.460
it shows us exactly the masked information and works in a manner which we are expecting.

249
00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:45.630
In this part of texture optimizations, I'd like to talk about texture compression,

250
00:18:45.630 --> 00:18:49.789
as every texture you import into Unreal Engine 5 is compressed in some way.

251
00:18:51.210 --> 00:18:54.809
Compression helps to reduce the overall memory footprint of your project,

252
00:18:54.809 --> 00:18:58.890
which is especially important for optimizing performance across all platforms.

253
00:18:59.529 --> 00:19:05.529
By default, most textures use either DTX1 or DTX5 compression formats.

254
00:19:05.529 --> 00:19:08.170
DTX1 is used for textures without an alpha channel,

255
00:19:08.170 --> 00:19:11.210
and DTX5 is used for textures that include an alpha channel.

256
00:19:11.849 --> 00:19:16.970
The compression types strike a good balance between visual quality and memory efficiency.

257
00:19:17.849 --> 00:19:21.049
Remember that changing compression settings for a texture will also affect

258
00:19:21.049 --> 00:19:23.049
other related settings automatically.

259
00:19:23.049 --> 00:19:26.009
For example, normal maps use the special compression type that

260
00:19:26.009 --> 00:19:30.009
disables sRGB since gamma correction isn't needed for normal data.

261
00:19:31.759 --> 00:19:38.079
Unreal Engine 5 is also set up in a way that is able to detect the type of texture you're importing.

262
00:19:38.079 --> 00:19:41.680
This means that it will automatically apply the appropriate compression settings

263
00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:44.400
for things like normal maps once they've been imported.

264
00:19:45.039 --> 00:19:47.920
If you ever need a texture to remain completely uncompressed,

265
00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:52.079
you can change the compression setting to user interface or even vector displacement.

266
00:19:52.079 --> 00:19:56.960
However, use this sparingly as uncompressed textures consume much more memory

267
00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:59.440
and can very negatively impact performance,

268
00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.400
especially on lower-end or memory-limited platforms.

269
00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:06.079
Making sure that each texture has compression is one of the easiest

270
00:20:06.079 --> 00:20:10.720
and most efficient ways to optimize performance without sacrificing visual quality,

271
00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:13.440
so it's worth understanding how all of these options work.

272
00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:17.529
With each of the compression settings you see here,

273
00:20:17.529 --> 00:20:21.609
you can actively set them and see the results instantly happen inside of the editor.

274
00:20:23.099 --> 00:20:27.180
In this section of texture optimizations, I'd like to talk about texture groups.

275
00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:32.059
Texture groups in Unreal help control how textures are displayed and managed by the GPU.

276
00:20:32.059 --> 00:20:36.779
They determine several important factors that directly impact both performance and visual quality.

277
00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:41.339
Texture groups decide which MIP or LOD resolution will be shown on screen,

278
00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:46.940
and this ensures that textures automatically scale in detail based on distance or performance targets.

279
00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:51.259
Secondly, they help define what type of filtering should be applied when a texture is

280
00:20:51.259 --> 00:20:56.859
minified or magnified by the GPU. In other words, when it's scaled up or down on screen,

281
00:20:56.859 --> 00:21:00.720
how is it going to react? Third, they control how the GPU blends

282
00:21:00.720 --> 00:21:04.319
two MIP levels when you view the surface from a grazing angle.

283
00:21:04.319 --> 00:21:09.039
This helps prevent visual artifacts and ensures smoother transition between MIP levels.

284
00:21:10.240 --> 00:21:14.400
Every texture in your project should be assigned to an appropriate texture group.

285
00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:19.039
These groups are organized by texture type, making it easy to map textures to categories.

286
00:21:19.039 --> 00:21:22.240
For example, normal maps for objects in the environment should use

287
00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:26.160
world normal. Normal maps for characters should use character normal.

288
00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:30.960
Using the right texture group ensures that textures behave exactly as expected,

289
00:21:30.960 --> 00:21:35.839
look correct under different viewing conditions, and perform efficiently on your target platform.
